    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-20884",
      "problem": "Plot the graph of $y = -\\left( \\dfrac 12 \\right)^x + 2.$",
      "worked_solution": "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14216573/assets/e9331b26e26b99d3d8fcaea0bb7864b45f697211d3f30c41e9cc5329b1ef5d45.png)\n\n- Reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14216573/assets/df29fef3da108d38afc6a0275cc8b0f52237915576a8d7528f90ffeab5588264.png)\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $2$ units up to give $y=-\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x}+2.$ The point $(0,-1)$ moves to $(0,1)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from from $y=0$ to $y=2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14216573/assets/e63aa682e19fef317badd419805ffd82a449559a159a41650d2260daf33eff76.png)",
